WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.474
Yes, Python has a ternary operator.

00:00:04.474 --> 00:00:11.940
The&nbsp;ternary operator in many languages since 1960 
has looked something like&nbsp;this, using the question mark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.560
But Python is way too cool for that.&nbsp;
So in Python, it's written using "if"

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.640
And to top it all off, it's not even referred to&nbsp;
as a ternary operator, even though it totally is!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.800
Instead, an expression like this in Python

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:32.034
"a if some condition else b" is technically&nbsp;
referred to as a conditional expression.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.480
But anyone will know what you mean if you&nbsp;
say ternary operator or if expression.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.813
Side note: Why is it called ternary&nbsp;
and not trinary?

00:00:39.813 --> 00:00:42.331
I don't know, maybe if 
you're an English expert,

00:00:42.331 --> 00:00:45.300
you can&nbsp;comment down 
below, and I'll pin the answer.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.672
The way it works is it 
first evaluates&nbsp;the condition,

00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:50.963
and then if it's true, gives you the 
first thing; otherwise, the&nbsp;second thing.

00:00:50.963 --> 00:00:53.448
This is kind of out of 
order of the way that it's written.

00:00:53.448 --> 00:00:57.420
But it's more in line&nbsp;with how 
you might say it out loud in English.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.100
The value is "x" if "x" is bigger&nbsp;
than 0, otherwise, negative "x."

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.135
A lot of different alternatives were
 considered,&nbsp;but they literally held a vote.

00:01:05.135 --> 00:01:09.004
And this "if" syntax won 
out over using a question mark.

00:01:09.004 --> 00:01:12.000
But&nbsp;importantly, the condition 
is the first thing that's evaluated.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.509
And the expression that's&nbsp;
not returned doesn't get evaluated at all

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:19.860
which is important in a case like this&nbsp;
where we don't want to divide by 0.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.760
It's a similar idea as&nbsp;
short-circuiting for "and" and "or."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.554
It's basically just syntactic sugar for a 
full&nbsp;"if" statement, but it also returns a value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.554 --> 00:01:31.239
And keep in mind that 
this is Python.

00:01:31.239 --> 00:01:36.720
And in&nbsp;Python, it usually doesn't check
 whether a value is literally "equals equals true."

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.989
Instead,&nbsp;Python uses its 
notion of truthiness

00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:42.000
where something like a non-empty 
list would be considered true,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.700
but an empty list would 
be considered false.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.602
In general, it's good for short definitions, like&nbsp;
setting the default value of a mutable argument.

00:01:49.602 --> 00:01:51.259
But do your best not 
to go overboard.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:56.031
It quickly becomes unreadable if you try 
to stuff too much stuff into one expression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.031 --> 00:01:59.322
Just like with any other operators,
 beware&nbsp;of operator precedence.

00:01:59.322 --> 00:02:02.639
Where you put or don't put 
parentheses can change the answer.

00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:06.249
The&nbsp;ternary operator has the lowest
 precedence of any operator in the language.

